<h2>Indian black pepper for export</h2>
<p>Malabar pepper carries one of the oldest reputations in the global spice trade, and while India no longer leads the world in pepper volume, it holds a premium position on quality. We source from Kerala — pepper's historic home — and from Karnataka's Wayanad and Kodagu districts, now the largest producing areas by volume, and supply garbled (MG1) and ungarbled grades matched to bulk density and moisture specification.</p>

<p>Harvest runs November-February. Berries are picked, dried and graded before moving to market, and whole peppercorns store considerably longer than most spices, which supports supply through much of the year even outside the harvest window.</p>

<h2>How should I specify black pepper?</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Garbled vs. ungarbled.</strong> Garbled pepper (often labelled MG1) has had light berries and foreign matter removed for a denser, more uniform, higher-value lot. Ungarbled skips that step at a lower price point.</li>
  <li><strong>Bulk density.</strong> The primary quality metric in the pepper trade — 500-560 g/l is typical for premium garbled grades, with higher density generally signalling denser, more mature, more pungent berries.</li>
  <li><strong>Moisture.</strong> Kept at or below 11-12% for stable, long-term storage without mould risk.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Which markets buy Indian black pepper?</h2>
<p>India's pepper buyers skew toward developed, quality-focused markets rather than the highest-volume commodity buyers — the United States leads, followed by Sweden, Germany, the UAE and Canada. See the cited breakdown below.</p>

<h2>Black pepper export pricing</h2>
<p>Pepper pricing reflects bulk density grade, garbling, and current Kerala/Karnataka crop conditions. Send your required grade, quantity and destination port and we'll quote current FOB/CIF terms.</p>

